{"key": "gr-qc/9701064#eq2-einstein", "name": "HPS eq. (2) metric and its Einstein-tensor left sides G^t_t, G^l_l, G^th_th", "source": {"located": "arXiv:gr-qc/9701064v1 (30 Jan 1997; preprint dated 23 Oct 1996), D. Hochberg, A. Popov, S. V. Sushkov, 'Self-consistent Wormhole Solutions of Semiclassical Gravity', Phys. Rev. Lett. 78, 2050 (1997). Eq. (2) and the three Einstein-tensor components on p.4; eqs. (5)-(7) pp.4-5; eq. (8) p.6; eq. (9) p.7; footnote [19] (units, MTW sign conventions) p.9.", "read_status": "READ", "via": "The arXiv v1 PDF text layer, pp.1-12 in full, as returned by alphaXiv answer_pdf_queries(paper='gr-qc/9701064') EARLIER IN THIS SAME SESSION (workflow wf_091111bd-d2a, transcript agent-a272945a7fe3bd7d8.jsonl), recovered verbatim from the session transcript to /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-Claude-Method-Works/6d820e7d-6d3c-5ac7-8067-527dc14c2847/scratchpad/d67/src/hps/hps_0.xml. This audit's own alphaXiv calls (answer_pdf_queries, get_paper_content, discover_papers) all returned 'quota exceeded'; arxiv.org, export.arxiv.org, link.aps.org, researchgate.net, scholar.archive.org and kpfu.ru (which hosts the PRL PDF) were EGRESS_BLOCKED. The journal PRL typesetting was NOT read (neither by this audit nor, per hpscentre.py:115-116, by the tree)."}, "published_statement": "p.4: 'The metric for a general static and spherically symmetric spacetime can be cast into the form ds^2 = -f(l)dt^2 + dl^2 + r^2(l)(dtheta^2 + sin^2 theta dphi^2), (2) where f(l), r(l) are two independent functions of the proper distance l. This form of the metric is suitable for dealing with wormholes, or for that matter, any static and spherically symmetric spacetime which might contain a throat, i.e., r(0) > 0. ... In the metric (2), the components of the Einstein tensor are given by G^t_t = 2r''/r + r'^2/r^2 - 1/r^2, G^l_l = f'r'/(fr) + r'^2/r^2 - 1/r^2, and G^theta_theta = f''/(2f) + r''/r + f'r'/(2fr) - f'^2/(4f^2); the prime denotes the derivative with respect to l.' The same three expressions are the printed left sides of eqs. (5), (6), (7). Footnote [19]: 'We use units such that hbar = c = G = 1 and our sign conventions follow those of C.W. Misner, K.S. Thorne and J.A. Wheeler, Gravitation'. The tree's hpscentre.py:422-424 is a character-for-character match of all three expressions.", "published_hypotheses": ["Static and spherically symmetric spacetime (p.4: 'a general static and spherically symmetric spacetime').", "Proper-distance radial gauge g_ll = 1 with f(l), r(l) 'two independent functions of the proper distance l' (p.4).", "f > 0 at least locally (p.6: 'f(0) > 0 (the other possibility, f(0) < 0, leads to a Euclidean metric)'); implicit also in the ln f of eqs. (5)-(7).", "MTW sign conventions and units hbar = c = G = 1 (footnote [19], p.9); G^mu_nu mixed, G^mu_nu = 8 pi <T^mu_nu> (eqs. (1), (3)).", "Context of use in HPS (not a hypothesis of the identity itself): a throat, r(0) > 0, r'(0) = 0 (p.4, p.6)."], "hypothesis_drift": ["ADDED (legitimate, checked): the tree applies eq. (2) and its Einstein tensor at a REGULAR CENTRE r(0) = 0 (hpscentre.py:40-46, smooth-centre data r = l + c3 l^3 + ..., f = f0(1 + b2 l^2 + ...)), whereas HPS use it at a throat r(0) > 0. The metric form (2) does not require r(0) > 0 (HPS: 'any static and spherically symmetric spacetime which might contain a throat'), but the printed components carry 1/r^2 and are finite at r = 0 only under smoothness data. The re-derivation shows that with the tree's data they are finite: G(0) = (18 c3, 2 b2 + 6 c3, 2 b2 + 6 c3), and the Misner-Sharp mass is m = -3 c3 l^3 + O(l^5), exactly hpscentre.py:46 / :291 (CENTRE_M_LEADING). No drift in the result; the smoothness data are a named input of the tree's use.", "OMITTED FROM THE CANONICAL HYPOTHESIS LIST (not from the code): the sign convention. G^a_b flips sign with the Ricci-sign convention; HPS fix it as MTW (footnote [19]). The canonical entry's hypothesis ('static spherical symmetry, proper-length radial gauge g = diag(-f, 1, r^2, r^2 sin^2 th)', from hpscentre.py:422-424 / 484-512) names no convention; the owner's curvature() (hpscentre.py:494-497, R^a_bcd = d_c Gam^a_bd - d_d Gam^a_bc + ..., Ric_bd = R^a_bad) IS the MTW convention, and the re-derivation confirms the sign physically (Einstein static universe gives G^t_t = -3/a^2, i.e. rho > 0). Recorded as a named hypothesis the entry should carry; nothing moves.", "OMITTED FROM THE CANONICAL HYPOTHESIS LIST (implicit in the code): f > 0. HPS state it (p.6). The tree's source terms carry ln f (hpscentre.py:350 onward, HPS eqs. (5)-(7)), so f > 0 is forced wherever the tree uses the system; the proper-length form also degenerates at a horizon (f -> 0), a region no tree run enters.", "OVER-STATEMENT IN THE SOURCE (a discrepancy, not a refutation; not used by the tree): HPS say 'a GENERAL static and spherically symmetric spacetime can be cast into the form (2)'. The form (2) with f > 0 covers only the static region outside any Killing horizon; HPS themselves report solutions 'with horizons located far from the throat' (p.8), at which (2) degenerates. The tree uses (2) only where f > 0."], "data_at_publication": [{"quantity": "any numerical input to eq. (2) or its Einstein tensor", "value_then": "none: eq. (2) is a gauge-fixed metric ansatz and G^t_t, G^l_l, G^th_th are a curvature identity of it (HPS p.4)", "value_now": "none", "source_now": "n/a -- the identity is re-derived symbolically in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-Claude-Method-Works/6d820e7d-6d3c-5ac7-8067-527dc14c2847/scratchpad/d67/rederive/gr-qc_9701064_eq2-einstein.py", "moves_conclusion": "no -- nothing measurable enters"}, {"quantity": "units / conventions", "value_then": "hbar = c = G = 1, MTW signs (HPS footnote [19])", "value_now": "unchanged (conventions, not data)", "source_now": "HPS footnote [19]; hpscentre.py uses Planck units (K in l_P, K_in_planck())", "moves_conclusion": "no"}, {"quantity": "control only: throat radii from eq. (8) built on G^l_l(throat) = -1/r0^2 with K^2 = 1/(5760 pi) (Planck units; K^2 is its own canonical key gr-qc/9701064#k2)", "value_then": "HPS: r(0) ~ 0.02 l_P at ln f(0) = -2/3 (p.7); r(0) ~ 67 l_P at f(0) = f''(0) = 1, r''(0) = 0 (p.7)", "value_now": "recomputed here: 0.0243 l_P and 67.260 l_P", "source_now": "computed in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-Claude-Method-Works/6d820e7d-6d3c-5ac7-8067-527dc14c2847/scratchpad/d67/rederive/gr-qc_9701064_eq2-einstein.py", "moves_conclusion": "no -- agrees with HPS's rounded figures; these are Planck-unit numbers, so no measured constant moves them"}], "rederivation": {"method": "sympy", "script_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-Claude-Method-Works/6d820e7d-6d3c-5ac7-8067-527dc14c2847/scratchpad/d67/rederive/gr-qc_9701064_eq2-einstein.py", "outcome": "ALL 24 CHECKS PASS (exit 0, ~3 s). Independent of hpscentre.py:curvature() (not imported). (1) Route A, coordinate Christoffel -> Riemann -> Ricci in MTW conventions: G^mu_nu of metric (2) is diagonal, G^ph_ph = G^th_th, and G^t_t, G^l_l, G^th_th equal HPS's printed p.4 expressions identically (difference simplifies to 0 for arbitrary f(l), r(l)). (2) Route B, orthonormal-tetrad sectional curvatures with f = e^{2 Phi}: agrees with route A for all three. (3) Contracted Bianchi identity d_l G^l_l + (f'/2f)(G^l_l - G^t_t) + (2r'/r)(G^l_l - G^th_th) = 0 identically; three controls (drop -f'^2/4f^2, drop f'r'/2fr, add a stray 1/r^2) each FAIL it, so the check discriminates. (4) Sign: Einstein static universe r = a sin(l/a) gives G^t_t = -3/a^2 (rho > 0 under MTW). (5) Ellis wormhole r = sqrt(l^2+b^2): (G^t_t, G^l_l, G^th_th) = (b^2, -b^2, b^2)/r^4, the textbook rho < 0 wormhole. (6) Schwarzschild exterior written in proper length (dr/dl = sqrt(1-2M/r)): all three vanish. (7) Regular centre r = l + c3 l^3, f = f0(1 + b2 l^2): G finite, G(0) = (18 c3, 2 b2 + 6 c3, 2 b2 + 6 c3), Misner-Sharp m = -3 c3 l^3 + O(l^5) as hpscentre.py:46 states. (8) Every term scales as length^-2. (9) HPS eq. (6) at l = 0, r' = f' = 0 (RHS transcribed from the page text) reproduces HPS's printed quartic (8) term for term, with G^l_l(throat) = -1/r0^2; eq. (9)'s r(0) = sqrt(-16 K^2 ln f(0)) is a root; numerics 0.0243 l_P and 67.260 l_P match HPS's '~0.02 l_P' and '~67 l_P'. Also run read-only against the tree: hpscentre.build + curvature gives [0,0,0] residual and divergence 0 (matches the owner's selftest line hpscentre.py:1233-1235). Two of my own expected values were first coded wrongly (Ellis G^t_t sign; regular-centre G^t_t sign) and failed; both were my errors (G^t_t = -8 pi rho), corrected, and are recorded here rather than hidden.", "agrees_with_source": "yes"}, "later_literature": [{"ref": "A. A. Popov, hep-th/0302039 (v2)", "effect": "extends", "what": "Independent second printing of the AHS-type source (Appendix B, (B1)-(B3)) against which the tree tests HPS eqs. (5)-(7); it concerns the right-hand side, not the Einstein side audited here. Per the tree, its asymptotic-flatness hypothesis is where the AHS approximation's domain enters (hpscentre.py:92-95, 118-122).", "read_status": "NAMED-NOT-READ in this audit (alphaXiv quota); recorded READ by the tree at hpscentre.py:118-122"}, {"ref": "E.-A. Kontou, arXiv:2405.05963, Sec. 5.2", "effect": "contested", "what": "Doubts that HPS's Planck-to-10^2 l_P throat lies in the semiclassical regime and asks for a QEI check. Bears on the physical use of HPS's solutions, not on eq. (2) or its Einstein tensor.", "read_status": "NAMED-NOT-READ in this audit; quoted as READ in full text by the tree at qeihps.py:22-37"}, {"ref": "A. A. Popov and S. V. Sushkov, gr-qc/0009028, 'Vacuum polarization of a scalar field in wormhole spacetimes'", "effect": "extends", "what": "Later work by two of the HPS authors in the same static spherical wormhole setting (title-level identification from a web search result).", "read_status": "NAMED-NOT-READ"}, {"ref": "R. Garattini and F. S. N. Lobo, gr-qc/0701020, 'Self sustained phantom wormholes in semi-classical gravity'", "effect": "extends", "what": "Later self-sustained semiclassical wormhole construction on a static spherically symmetric metric (title-level identification only).", "read_status": "NAMED-NOT-READ"}, {"ref": "arXiv:2506.21256, 'Peculiarities of quantum field theory in the presence of a wormhole'; arXiv:2102.03544, 'Reissner-Nordstrom geometry counterpart in semiclassical gravity'", "effect": "extends", "what": "Recent semiclassical work on static spherical geometries surfaced by the later-literature search (title-level identification only). Nothing found that narrows or contradicts eq. (2)'s Einstein tensor, and nothing could: it is an identity, re-derived here two ways.", "read_status": "NAMED-NOT-READ"}], "lacked_data": "M's hypothesis does not bite on this result, and the evidence runs against it here, not for it. Eq. (2) is a gauge choice (proper radial distance) for a static spherical metric, and G^t_t, G^l_l, G^th_th are its Einstein tensor -- a curvature identity with no empirical input at all (no constant, no measurement, no fit). There is no datum HPS could have lacked that would change it; it was derived here two independent ways (coordinate and tetrad), passes the Bianchi identity with discriminating controls, reproduces three textbook solutions (Einstein static, Ellis, Schwarzschild in proper length), and reproduces HPS's own eq. (8) and the two throat radii they print. What HPS lacked is relevant ELSEWHERE, not here: an exact <T_ab> on these metrics (the AHS source is an approximation; hpscentre.py:36-38), and any check of the semiclassical regime at Planck-scale throats (Kontou 2405.05963) -- those bear on the right-hand side and the domain, which are separate canonical keys. Two source-side discrepancies were found in passing and are recorded, not repaired: (i) HPS's 'general static and spherically symmetric spacetime' over-states the reach of (2), which degenerates at horizons; (ii) OUT OF THIS KEY'S SCOPE, footnote [22] derives f'(0) = 0 from r'(0) = 0 by 'the chain rule' r'(l) df/dr = f'(l), which presumes df/dr is finite at the throat -- a hypothesis stated as a consequence; HPS's own reflection symmetry f(l) = f(-l) (p.6) supplies f'(0) = 0 independently, so their data (9) and the tree's use of them are unaffected.", "grade": "STANDS", "grade_evidence": "Statement READ at source (arXiv v1 text layer p.4, via this session's earlier alphaXiv return): the metric and all three Einstein components match hpscentre.py:422-424 character for character. Re-derived independently by sympy two ways (coordinate Christoffels in MTW conventions; orthonormal-tetrad sectional curvatures with f = e^{2 Phi}), both agreeing identically for arbitrary f(l), r(l); Bianchi identity holds and three perturbed controls fail it; sign fixed physically (Einstein static universe rho > 0); Schwarzschild-in-proper-length vacuum, Ellis wormhole and regular-centre limits all come out right; HPS eq. (8) is reproduced from eq. (6)'s left side at the throat, with HPS's printed radii (0.0243 vs '~0.02' l_P; 67.26 vs '~67' l_P). No datum enters, so none can move. The tree's one extension (use at a regular centre r(0) = 0, hpscentre.py:40-46) is within the metric form's reach and checked finite, with m = -3 c3 l^3 as the tree states. The canonical entry omits two hypotheses the owner's code already satisfies (MTW sign convention; f > 0) -- recorded as naming gaps, not as narrowing, since no use of the tree falls outside them.", "what_would_change_the_grade": "Only a source-reading discrepancy could: the JOURNAL PRL 78, 2050 text was not read (by this audit or the tree), and if its printed Einstein components differed from the arXiv v1 text layer, the tree's label 'HPS's printed left sides' would become a statement about the preprint only -- the mathematics (the Einstein tensor of metric (2)) would still STAND, as it is re-derived here. Use of the tree's system at f <= 0 or at a horizon would be a NARROWING (the proper-length form does not cover it); no current run does so. A reader should also re-run this audit's own alphaXiv read directly once the quota resets, since the page text used here was recovered from an earlier call in the same session rather than fetched by this audit.", "reverify_command": "python3 /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-Claude-Method-Works/6d820e7d-6d3c-5ac7-8067-527dc14c2847/scratchpad/d67/rederive/gr-qc_9701064_eq2-einstein.py   # exits 0 on ALL CHECKS PASS; source page text at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-Claude-Method-Works/6d820e7d-6d3c-5ac7-8067-527dc14c2847/scratchpad/d67/src/hps/hps_0.xml (p.4 = eq. (2) and the Einstein tensor)", "report_path": "/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-Claude-Method-Works/6d820e7d-6d3c-5ac7-8067-527dc14c2847/scratchpad/d67/audits/gr-qc_9701064_eq2-einstein.json"}
